guide('VCF 9 ESXi monitoring and troubleshooting commands', 'VMware VCF', 'Infrastructure',
 'Inspect ESXi 9 identity, resources, networking, storage and time when investigating a VCF workload issue.',
 'ESXi 9 host in a VCF 9 environment. Commands are read-only inspection examples; privileges and available namespaces vary by release.',
 'Use authorized ESXi Shell or SSH access to the affected host. Confirm the management context and maintenance policy before collecting output; host information and logs can contain sensitive data.',
 [('Broadcom ESXCLI storage reference','https://developer.broadcom.com/xapis/esxcli-command-reference/latest/namespace/esxcli_storage.html'),('Broadcom ESXCLI network reference','https://developer.broadcom.com/xapis/esxcli-command-reference/latest/namespace/esxcli_network.html'),('Broadcom ESXCLI system reference','https://developer.broadcom.com/xapis/esxcli-command-reference/latest/namespace/esxcli_system.html')], [
 ('vmware -v', 'Identify the hypervisor version', 'Record the installed ESXi product version and build. Compare the host with the VCF supported bill of materials before interpreting a command result or planning a change.'),
 ('esxcli system uptime get', 'Inspect host uptime', 'Compare the uptime with the incident timeline and planned maintenance. A recent reboot can explain an interruption but does not establish its cause; correlate events and logs.'),
 ('esxcli network ip interface list', 'Inspect VMkernel interfaces', 'Identify management, vMotion and storage VMkernel interfaces and their state. Compare MTU and service topology with the design; an interface being enabled does not prove end-to-end connectivity.'),
 ('esxcli network nic list', 'Inspect physical network adapters', 'Compare link status and speed with the intended uplinks and switch ports. A down link can be expected for an unused adapter; verify the host design before treating it as a fault.'),
 ('esxcli storage filesystem list', 'Inspect mounted filesystems and capacity', 'Find the datastore and compare mount state, free space and total size. A mounted datastore can still experience latency or path failures; correlate with storage paths and vCenter performance data.'),
 ('esxcli storage core path list', 'Inspect storage path states', 'Compare device identifiers, adapters and path states with the expected multipath design. An inactive standby path may be intentional; inspect the path policy and array design before drawing conclusions.'),
 ('esxtop', 'Observe live host resource usage', 'Run interactively and consult the ESXi version-specific documentation for display keys and counters. Compare sustained CPU, memory, disk and network pressure with a baseline; one sample does not establish the source of a VM performance issue.')],
 ('A datastore workload becomes slow','A VM on one host shows storage latency while other services remain online.',[(0,'Record the host version and build for correlation with the VCF inventory.'),(4,'Check that the relevant datastore is mounted and has available capacity.'),(5,'Compare storage path states with the approved multipath design.'),(6,'Observe sustained storage latency and resource behavior during an approved incident window.')],
 'Example: the datastore remains mounted, but a required path is unavailable and latency is elevated over several samples.', 'A path issue is a plausible contributor, but a mounted datastore or one inactive path alone does not prove root cause. Correlate array health, path policy and latency across hosts.', 'After the storage owner resolves the approved issue, compare path state and performance over time and verify the affected workload.', 'Provide host build, datastore identifier, timestamps, path states and performance samples to the virtualization and storage teams.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-03'

guide('VCF 9 vCenter appliance root password recovery', 'VMware VCF', 'Infrastructure',
 'Reset a forgotten vCenter Server Appliance root password through an authorized SSO administrator and reconcile the VCF credential.',
 'vCenter Server Appliance 9.x in VCF. This changes the vCenter appliance OS root account, not an ESXi host root or the SSO administrator password.',
 'Confirm the exact vCenter and approved maintenance process. Take a current backup and snapshot as required by Broadcom; for Enhanced Linked Mode follow its coordinated backup or powered-off snapshot guidance. The no-reboot method requires SSH access and an SSO user in SystemConfiguration.BashShellAdministrator; follow the linked Broadcom GRUB procedure if this access is unavailable.',
 [('Broadcom vCenter root reset without reboot','https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/321369/vcenter-server-appliance-root-password-r.html'),('Broadcom vCenter root recovery and GRUB procedure','https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/322247/change-or-reset-the-root-account-passwor.html'),('Broadcom VCF credential remediation','https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/408771/vcenter-root-account-showing-disconnect.html')], [
 ('ssh administrator@vsphere.local@vcsa.example.com', 'Connect as an authorized SSO administrator', 'Replace the SSO domain and appliance FQDN with your own. This is an SSH login to the vCenter appliance, and the user must have the documented BashShellAdministrator membership. SSH and shell access must be permitted by your environment.'),
 ('shell.set --enable true', 'Enable the appliance Bash shell if needed', 'Run in the vCenter appliance shell with an appropriately authorized SSO user. Broadcom documents this when shell access has not already been enabled. Follow local policy for any later change to the shell setting.'),
 ('shell', 'Enter the appliance Bash shell', 'Run this in the appliance shell after enabling shell access if necessary. Confirm the prompt and appliance identity before running the following commands; these commands target the vCenter appliance itself.'),
 ('sudo /usr/sbin/faillock --user root --reset', 'Unlock root only if its account is locked', 'Use this conditional step for a root account locked after unsuccessful login attempts. Broadcom specifies faillock for current Photon-based vCenter releases. An expired or forgotten password does not necessarily require clearing lockout.'),
 ('sudo passwd root', 'Set a new vCenter appliance root password', 'This changes the root password. Enter and confirm the new password interactively and check the success message; do not place passwords in commands, recordings or tickets. Coordinate the new credential with VCF password management.'),
 ('sudo chage -l root', 'Review the root password expiration details', 'After the password change, review the account expiration and password-aging fields. This inspects the existing policy; do not disable expiration merely as a recovery shortcut. Verify a root login through an approved access path.')],
 ('vCenter appliance root is locked after failed logins','A VCF-managed vCenter 9 appliance rejects root login, while an authorized SSO administrator retains SSH and shell privileges.',[(0,'Confirm the target vCenter and log in with the permitted SSO administrator.'),(1,'Enable the Bash shell if it is not already enabled, according to the access policy.'),(2,'Enter Bash and verify that the shell is running on the intended appliance.'),(3,'If lockout is confirmed, reset the root failure counter before changing the password.'),(4,'Set and confirm the new root password interactively, then check that the command reports success.')],
 'Example: the authorized SSO shell is available, the root account was locked, and the password update completes successfully.',
 'The reset restores the appliance root credential; it does not change ESXi host passwords or automatically update the stored VCF management credential. If SSO shell access is unavailable, use the separate reboot and GRUB recovery procedure in the linked Broadcom article.',
 'Verify root login using the approved access path, review expiration, and use the supported VCF Operations password-management remediation workflow to reconcile the stored vCenter root credential. Confirm the account returns to Active; Broadcom notes status updates can take time.',
 'Escalate unavailable SSO or console access, linked-mode backup questions, or failed credential reconciliation to the VCF administrator and Broadcom support. Preserve task IDs and sanitized logs without sharing passwords.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-03'

guide('Ubiquiti UniFi USW Layer 3 discovery and adoption', 'Ubiquiti', 'Networking',
 'Discover and adopt a UniFi USW switch when the UniFi Network application is on another subnet or site.',
 'UniFi USW switches and UniFi Network. Layer 3 discovery means management adoption across routed networks; it does not require the switch itself to support Layer 3 routing.',
 'Use the correct UniFi site and an authorized administrator. Identify the switch management VLAN, IP address and current ownership before changing its inform target. A new or explicitly reset device can be adopted; do not factory-reset or take over an already managed production switch without an approved migration.',
 [('Ubiquiti remote adoption (Layer 3)','https://help.ui.com/hc/en-us/articles/204909754-Remote-Adoption-Layer-3'),('Ubiquiti device adoption','https://help.ui.com/hc/en-us/articles/360012622613-UniFi-Device-Adoption'),('Ubiquiti device SSH access','https://help.ui.com/hc/en-us/articles/204909374-Connecting-to-UniFi-with-Debug-Tools-SSH'),('Ubiquiti Layer 3 routing','https://help.ui.com/hc/en-us/articles/360042281174-Layer-3-Routing')], [
 ('Identify the USW and its management network', 'Confirm switch identity, ownership and addressing', 'Record model, MAC address, management IP, subnet, gateway and current adoption state. Check the DHCP lease or network inventory if the address is unknown. A switch already controlled by another application needs an approved migration, not an immediate reset.'),
 ('Verify the UniFi Network application and site', 'Select the correct controller and inform address', 'Confirm the intended UniFi Network application is running and that you are viewing the correct site. Identify its address reachable from the switch. For Official UniFi Hosting use the dashboard Copy Inform URL rather than guessing a hostname.'),
 ('Test-NetConnection unifi.example.com -Port 8080', 'Test the inform TCP port from an equivalent network', 'Run this PowerShell example from a Windows test host on the same management VLAN and with equivalent firewall policy as the switch; replace the hostname. TcpTestSucceeded confirms only that test host can reach TCP 8080, not that the switch can. Check routing, ACLs and return traffic for the actual switch.'),
 ('ssh <device-user>@<switch-ip>', 'Connect to the USW management interface', 'Replace both placeholders and use the authorized UniFi device SSH credentials. Device SSH credentials differ from UniFi Console SSH credentials. The initial SSH example applies to a factory-default device in Ubiquiti’s remote adoption workflow; follow your approved migration process for an existing managed switch.'),
 ('set-inform http://unifi.example.com:8080/inform', 'Point the switch at the UniFi Network application', 'Run this command in the USW SSH session, substituting the application IP or FQDN reachable from that switch. It changes the device inform target. For Official UniFi Hosting use its provided inform URL. Do not use the gateway IP unless it actually hosts or forwards the UniFi application.'),
 ('UniFi Network → Devices → Ready to Adopt → Adopt', 'Adopt and verify the intended switch', 'In the intended site, match the device MAC and choose Adopt. Ubiquiti notes that set-inform may need repeating after the device appears. Wait for provisioning and confirm the switch remains connected; an inform command alone does not complete adoption.'),
 ('DHCP Option 43 or DNS unifi', 'Review alternate discovery methods', 'For scaled deployments, Ubiquiti documents DHCP Option 43 and DNS resolution of unifi to the Network application. Check the options supplied on the switch management VLAN and the vendor-specific encoding. The device may try multiple inform sources, so remove unintended competing targets through an approved change.'),
 ('Compare routed adoption and Layer 3 switching', 'Distinguish controller discovery from packet routing', 'Discovery across subnets needs a reachable application and routed TCP 8080. Inter-VLAN routing on the switch is a separate feature available only on selected USW models; consult Ubiquiti’s Layer 3 routing guide if the switch should route client VLANs.')],
 ('A branch switch does not appear in the central controller',
  'A new factory-default USW has a management address on a branch VLAN. UniFi Network runs on a different subnet.',
  [(0,'Match the switch MAC to its management address and confirm that it is available for adoption.'),
   (1,'Identify the intended Network site and the application address reachable from the branch.'),
   (2,'Test TCP 8080 from the switch management path or an equivalent authorized test host. In this example the equivalent test initially times out.'),
   (3,'After the network team corrects the approved route or firewall policy, connect to the switch with the authorized device SSH account.'),
   (4,'Set the correct inform URL on the USW and watch the intended application for its MAC address.'),
   (5,'Adopt the switch in the intended site and repeat set-inform only if the device appears but requires the documented retry.')],
  'Illustrative findings: the switch has a valid management IP; the controller is on another subnet; TCP 8080 initially times out and succeeds after an approved firewall correction.',
  'The timeout supports a connectivity investigation. A test from another host is supporting evidence, not proof of the switch path. Once connectivity works, check ownership, inform address and competing DHCP or DNS discovery settings if adoption still fails.',
  'Confirm the correct MAC appears in the intended site, adoption and provisioning complete, and the switch remains connected. Verify management reachability and the expected port configuration after provisioning.',
  'Provide switch MAC, model, management VLAN/IP, application FQDN, inform URL without credentials, TCP 8080 test source and timestamps to the network or UniFi administrator. Avoid factory reset until ownership and an approved recovery path are established.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['kind']='Step'
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-03'

guide('Kubernetes service endpoint troubleshooting', 'Kubernetes', 'Cloud',
 'Trace a Service through selectors, pod readiness, EndpointSlices and service ports.',
 'Kubernetes with EndpointSlice support and kubectl read access to the target namespace.',
 'Replace production, web, app=web and the pod name. Confirm the context and use an authorized account; these commands inspect resources without changing them.',
 [('Kubernetes service debugging','https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-service/'),('Kubernetes EndpointSlices','https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/endpoint-slices/')], [
 ('kubectl config current-context','Confirm the cluster context','Compare the selected context with the approved cluster inventory. Context names can be arbitrary local labels, so verify the endpoint through your organization’s inventory before interpreting production results.'),
 ('kubectl get service web -n production -o wide','Inspect Service addresses and ports','Record the ClusterIP, service ports and selector for the affected Service. A ClusterIP being assigned does not prove that any backend is ready or that the application is listening on its target port.'),
 ('kubectl describe service web -n production','Inspect Service selector and endpoint summary','Compare the selector, port and targetPort with the deployed application. A named target port must match the pod’s declared container port name; the summary is a starting point, not a substitute for inspecting EndpointSlices.'),
 ('kubectl get pods -n production -l app=web -o wide --show-labels','Inspect selected pods and readiness','Use the selector actually shown by the Service rather than assuming app=web. Check labels, readiness and node placement; a Running pod can still be unready or expose the wrong application port.'),
 ('kubectl get endpointslice -n production -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web -o yaml','Inspect EndpointSlice addresses and conditions','Compare endpoint addresses, ports and readiness conditions with the intended pods. A missing or unready endpoint narrows the investigation to selection or readiness; avoid sharing full resource output if it contains internal addresses.')],
 ('A Service has no ready backends','Clients receive errors from a ClusterIP even though the web pods are running.',[(0,'Verify the cluster context and production namespace before inspecting resources.'),(2,'Read the Service selector and target port from the actual Service definition.'),(3,'Compare selected pod labels and readiness against the selector.'),(4,'Inspect EndpointSlices for ready addresses corresponding to those pods.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the Service selects app=web, but the deployed pods have app=frontend and the EndpointSlice has no ready backends.',
 'A selector mismatch explains the empty backend list in this example. An empty list can also reflect pod readiness or other Service configuration, so inspect those separately.',
 'After the workload owner corrects the selector or pod labels through the approved deployment, confirm ready EndpointSlices and test the Service from an authorized client.',
 'Send the namespace, Service selector, pod labels, endpoint conditions and timestamps to the application platform owner.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-07'

guide('Docker network connectivity inspection', 'Docker', 'Infrastructure',
 'Inspect container network membership, addressing, port publication and bounded application logs.',
 'Docker Engine CLI against the intended local or remote Docker daemon; network behavior differs for Docker Desktop and Linux hosts.',
 'Replace web and app-net with exact container and network names. Confirm the Docker context first; inspect output can contain internal addresses and configuration.',
 [('Docker network inspect','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/network/inspect/'),('Docker network list','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/network/ls/'),('Docker container inspect','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/inspect/'),('Docker published ports','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/port/')], [
 ('docker context show','Confirm the Docker context','Record the active context and check it against the incident environment. A similarly named container can exist on another daemon; endpoint overrides can also affect where the CLI connects.'),
 ('docker network ls','List available Docker networks','Find the intended network name, ID and driver. A network existing on the daemon does not prove that the application containers are attached or that their services are responding.'),
 ('docker network inspect app-net','Inspect a network and attached containers','Compare the network ID, driver, subnet and listed container endpoints with the intended deployment. Treat IP addresses as implementation details that can change when containers are recreated.'),
 ('docker inspect --format "{{json .NetworkSettings.Networks}}" web','Inspect one container’s network attachments','Compare the exact container’s network names and addresses with the peer’s attachments. A shared network enables a possible path but does not establish application readiness or listening ports.'),
 ('docker port web','Inspect published host ports','Check the host address and port mapped to the container. Published host ports and container-to-container ports are separate paths; a mapping does not prove that the process inside is listening.'),
 ('docker logs --since 30m --tail 100 --timestamps web','Review a bounded application log sample','Compare connection and startup errors with the incident time. Logging drivers and retention affect what is available; redact credentials and application data before sharing any excerpts.')],
 ('A frontend cannot reach its backend container','A frontend and backend work individually, but requests between them fail after a deployment.',[(0,'Confirm the CLI targets the daemon running the affected workload.'),(1,'List the networks and identify the expected application network.'),(2,'Inspect the network membership for the frontend and backend.'),(3,'Compare the exact attachments of the affected container with its peer.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the frontend is attached to app-net but the backend was recreated on another network.',
 'The membership mismatch is a supported explanation for the missing shared network path. Service name resolution and the backend listener still need separate verification.',
 'After the owner corrects the approved deployment configuration, inspect both attachments again and test the application through its normal path.',
 'Provide daemon context, container IDs, network IDs and sanitized error timestamps to the deployment owner.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-07'

guide('DNS record investigation with dig', 'BIND', 'Networking',
 'Compare DNS answers, authoritative servers, response codes and TCP fallback using dig.',
 'BIND 9 dig on a host with access to the intended DNS resolvers. Queries send DNS traffic.',
 'Replace app.example.com, example.com and 192.0.2.53 with approved names and resolvers. Compare results from the affected network; public and internal DNS can intentionally differ.',
 [('ISC BIND dig manual','https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/latest/manpages.html#dig-dns-lookup-utility')], [
 ('dig app.example.com A','Query an IPv4 host record','Read the response status, answer section, TTL and responding server. NXDOMAIN and a timeout are different observations. A successful A answer does not establish application or TLS health.'),
 ('dig app.example.com AAAA','Query the IPv6 host record','Compare any IPv6 answer with the application’s supported network path. No AAAA answer can be expected for an IPv4-only service; an answer alone does not prove IPv6 reachability.'),
 ('dig @192.0.2.53 app.example.com A','Query a chosen resolver','Replace the documentation address with an approved DNS server and compare its response with the default resolver. Differences can result from split DNS, caches or resolver configuration.'),
 ('dig example.com NS','Find nameservers advertised for a zone','Record the returned nameserver names and TTL, then verify authority for the actual zone with the DNS owner. An NS answer from a recursive resolver can itself be cached.'),
 ('dig +tcp @192.0.2.53 app.example.com A','Repeat the query over TCP','Compare the TCP result with the normal query to help investigate transport filtering or truncated responses. A successful TCP query does not prove that every UDP path or application resolver works.'),
 ('dig -x 192.0.2.25','Inspect reverse DNS for an address','Replace the documentation address with an authorized target. PTR data can be absent or independently managed and should not be assumed to match the forward record.')],
 ('Two clients receive different service addresses','An internal application resolves to different addresses from two client networks.',[(0,'Capture the default A answer and responding resolver from each client network.'),(2,'Query the intended internal resolver explicitly and record the answer and TTL.'),(1,'Check whether an IPv6 answer changes the application path.'),(4,'Compare TCP DNS behavior if normal queries time out or are truncated.')],
 'Illustrative findings: one resolver returns the intended internal address while another returns a public address for the same name.',
 'Different answers support investigating resolver selection or split-horizon DNS. They do not by themselves establish which answer is incorrect or whether a cache is stale.',
 'After the DNS owner applies the approved correction, repeat queries from the affected client and verify the application by its normal hostname.',
 'Provide query time, client network, resolver addresses, response codes and TTLs to the DNS owner.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-07'

guide('Windows TCP listener and process investigation', 'Windows', 'IT fundamentals',
 'Map a TCP listener to its owning process and compare local binding with a remote connectivity test.',
 'Windows PowerShell with the NetTCPIP module. Some process details require elevated read privileges.',
 'Replace port 443 and server.example.com with the approved service. Run listener checks on the server and the TCP probe on an authorized client; the probe sends traffic.',
 [('Microsoft Get-NetTCPConnection','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/get-nettcpconnection'),('Microsoft Test-NetConnection','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/test-netconnection')], [
 ('Get-NetTCPConnection -State Listen','List TCP listeners','Inspect local addresses, local ports and owning PIDs. A listener on loopback is not a listener on every interface. The command is a point-in-time view and does not list UDP sockets.'),
 ('Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 443 -State Listen','Inspect the service port','Replace 443 with the intended TCP port and compare the binding with the service design. No result can mean the process is not listening at that moment or that the wrong port was inspected.'),
 ('Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 443 -State Listen | Select-Object LocalAddress,LocalPort,OwningProcess','Capture listener address and process ID','Record the exact PID and address for the incident. An address of 127.0.0.1 is local-only; an all-interface binding is still subject to host and network policy.'),
 ('Get-Process -Id 1234','Identify the process owning the listener','Replace 1234 with the PID from the previous result. A PID can be reused after a process exits, so collect both observations close together and confirm the application owner.'),
 ('Get-NetTCPConnection -State Established','Inspect current established TCP connections','Compare local and remote endpoints with a known healthy baseline. Existing established connections do not establish that new clients can connect or that application authentication succeeds.'),
 ('Test-NetConnection server.example.com -Port 443 -InformationLevel Detailed','Test TCP from an affected client','Run from the client network and inspect TcpTestSucceeded, source address and interface. A successful TCP handshake does not validate HTTP, TLS certificates or user authorization.')],
 ('A web service works locally but remote users time out','A Windows-hosted web service answers on the server itself but clients cannot connect.',[(1,'Check the expected listener on the server and record the port.'),(2,'Inspect the local bind address and PID for that listener.'),(3,'Match the PID to the expected application process and confirm that it has not restarted between observations.'),(5,'Run the same TCP port test from an authorized affected client.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the expected process listens only on 127.0.0.1:443, while a remote client TCP test fails.',
 'The loopback-only binding is a supported explanation for remote connectivity failure. Check the application’s intended exposure and firewall path before changing any setting.',
 'After an approved binding correction, repeat the listener and client TCP checks, then verify the normal application and TLS flow.',
 'Share server identity, bind address, process ID, client source and test timestamps with the application owner.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-07'

guide('TLS certificate inspection with OpenSSL', 'OpenSSL', 'Cybersecurity',
 'Inspect the presented TLS certificate, hostname validation and certificate expiration using OpenSSL.',
 'OpenSSL 3.x CLI and an approved TLS endpoint. Examples assume a POSIX shell and a PEM certificate file.',
 'Replace app.example.com and server.pem with the authorized hostname and certificate file. The s_client commands contact the remote service. Never share private keys or paste secrets into commands.',
 [('OpenSSL s_client reference','https://docs.openssl.org/3.0/man1/openssl-s_client/'),('OpenSSL x509 reference','https://docs.openssl.org/3.0/man1/openssl-x509/')], [
 ('openssl version','Identify the OpenSSL version','Record the OpenSSL release and compare supported options with the installed manual. Trust-store configuration and TLS behavior can vary across builds and platforms.'),
 ('openssl s_client -connect app.example.com:443 -servername app.example.com -showcerts </dev/null','Inspect the server-presented certificate chain','Specify the hostname for SNI and inspect the returned certificates and handshake messages. A displayed chain is the server-provided chain, not necessarily a verified trust chain; output can include internal names.'),
 ('openssl s_client -connect app.example.com:443 -servername app.example.com -verify_hostname app.example.com -verify_return_error </dev/null','Check hostname and certificate validation','Use the expected DNS name for SNI and hostname verification. Trust depends on the OpenSSL trust store and any explicitly configured CA files; compare it with the client environment experiencing the issue.'),
 ('openssl x509 -in server.pem -noout -subject -issuer -dates','Inspect a saved certificate’s identity and dates','Read the subject, issuer and validity period from the public PEM certificate file. This command does not contact the server and cannot establish which certificate a load balancer is currently serving.'),
 ('openssl x509 -in server.pem -noout -ext subjectAltName','Inspect subject alternative names','Compare the DNS name clients use with the certificate subject alternative names. Hostname matching rules and wildcard scope matter; use explicit hostname verification as well as reading the extension.'),
 ('openssl x509 -in server.pem -checkend 604800 -noout','Check whether a certificate expires within seven days','The interval is in seconds. Use the command exit status with the output and confirm the file represents the certificate in question; this is not a complete chain or hostname validation.')],
 ('A browser reports a certificate name mismatch','Users reach an HTTPS endpoint but the browser rejects the presented certificate.',[(0,'Record the OpenSSL version and the hostname users actually enter.'),(1,'Inspect the certificate presented with the expected SNI hostname.'),(2,'Run explicit hostname verification using the same hostname.'),(4,'Compare the public certificate subject alternative names with the requested host.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the server presents a certificate for old.example.com and explicit verification for app.example.com fails.',
 'The observed hostname mismatch explains the verification failure for this connection. It does not establish whether DNS, load-balancer selection or certificate deployment caused the wrong certificate to be presented.',
 'After the certificate or endpoint owner corrects the approved configuration, repeat the hostname validation and test the normal browser path with trust checks enabled.',
 'Provide the hostname, endpoint address, certificate issuer, validity dates and validation error to the certificate or load-balancer owner; never include private keys.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-07'

guide('Windows route selection and VPN troubleshooting','Windows','Networking',
 'Inspect route selection, interface metrics and the source path for a destination on a Windows host.',
 'Windows 10/11 or Windows Server with NetTCPIP PowerShell module.',
 'Run on the affected host and replace 192.0.2.25 with the approved destination. VPN policy, route compartments and other filters can affect the observed path.',
 [('Microsoft Get-NetRoute','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/get-netroute'),('Microsoft Find-NetRoute','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/find-netroute'),('Microsoft Test-NetConnection','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/test-netconnection')],[
 ('Get-NetIPConfiguration','Inspect interface configuration','Identify active adapters, their IP addresses and gateways. VPN and physical adapters may both be present; note the interface associated with the affected traffic.'),
 ('Get-NetRoute -AddressFamily IPv4','List IPv4 routes','Compare destination prefixes, next hops, interface indexes and route metrics. The longest matching prefix matters before comparing metrics for otherwise equivalent routes.'),
 ('Get-NetIPInterface -AddressFamily IPv4','Inspect IPv4 interface metrics','Compare the interface metrics and connection states with the affected route. Automatic metrics and changes in VPN state can affect selection; capture output during the incident.'),
 ('Find-NetRoute -RemoteIPAddress 192.0.2.25','Find the selected local address and route','Replace the documentation address with the actual destination. Record the selected source address, route and interface, then check the return path separately.'),
 ('Test-NetConnection 192.0.2.25 -Port 443 -InformationLevel Detailed','Test an approved TCP service','Run from the affected host and inspect the selected source interface and TcpTestSucceeded. A TCP connection alone does not prove that TLS or the application works.')],
 ('VPN users reach the wrong path','A user cannot connect to an internal service after enabling a VPN.',[(0,'Record active addresses and gateways with the VPN connected.'),(1,'Compare specific destination prefixes and next hops in the IPv4 route table.'),(3,'Find the route and source address selected for the actual service address.'),(4,'Test the approved service port and record the client source interface.')],
 'Illustrative findings: a more-specific route points to the physical adapter rather than the expected VPN interface.',
 'The selected route explains the observed forward-path mismatch in this example. Verify the intended VPN configuration and return route before proposing a change.',
 'After the VPN owner applies an approved correction, repeat the route lookup and application test from the affected host.',
 'Provide the destination, route prefixes, interface indexes and incident time to the network team.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-10'


guide('Kubernetes deployment rollout investigation','Kubernetes','Cloud',
 'Inspect deployment rollout status, ReplicaSets, pod readiness and events during a stalled release.',
 'kubectl and read access to Deployments, ReplicaSets, Pods and Events in the target namespace.',
 'Replace production, web and app=web with the real namespace, Deployment and verified selector. These commands inspect resources and do not undo a rollout.',
 [('Kubernetes deployments','https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/'),('kubectl rollout status','https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_rollout/kubectl_rollout_status/')],[
 ('kubectl config current-context','Confirm the selected cluster','Compare the locally configured context with the approved cluster inventory before collecting evidence; similar context names can refer to different environments.'),
 ('kubectl get deployment web -n production -o wide','Inspect deployment replica counts','Compare desired, ready, up-to-date and available replicas with the intended rollout. A Deployment object existing does not prove all new pods are serving.'),
 ('kubectl rollout status deployment/web -n production --watch=false','Read current rollout status','This returns the current status without an ongoing watch. If the rollout changes, repeat the command and correlate the output with Deployment conditions and event timestamps.'),
 ('kubectl get replicasets -n production -l app=web','Compare ReplicaSet revisions','Use the actual Deployment selector, which may differ from this example. Compare desired, current and ready counts to distinguish old replicas from the newest rollout.'),
 ('kubectl get pods -n production -l app=web -o wide','Inspect selected pod state','Review readiness, restarts and node placement for the selected pods. Running is not equivalent to ready; inspect the specific pod further when status or readiness is unexpected.'),
 ('kubectl describe deployment web -n production','Read conditions and recent events','Compare available and progressing conditions and recent events with the rollout window. Events are temporary and can be aggregated; use centralized logs for older evidence.')],
 ('A rollout stalls with unavailable replicas','A new application release remains partially available after deployment.',[(0,'Verify the current context and intended namespace before inspecting the deployment.'),(1,'Record desired and available replica counts during the incident.'),(3,'Compare old and new ReplicaSet counts using the verified selector.'),(4,'Inspect readiness and restart status for the pods belonging to the new revision.')],
 'Illustrative findings: old replicas remain ready while the new ReplicaSet has zero ready pods.',
 'The rollout is waiting on new replicas in this example. Pod events and logs are needed to distinguish image, configuration, scheduling or readiness failures.',
 'After the application owner resolves the cause, repeat the rollout status and confirm expected available replicas and a normal application request.',
 'Send Deployment and ReplicaSet identities, conditions and sanitized event times to the platform owner.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-10'

guide('Docker Compose service state investigation','Docker','Infrastructure',
 'Inspect the effective Compose service model, container state and recent service logs.',
 'Docker Compose v2 in a trusted project directory, connected to the intended Docker daemon.',
 'Use the same Compose files, project name and environment as the deployment. Configuration and logs can expose secrets; these commands do not start, stop or recreate services.',
 [('Docker Compose config','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/compose/config/'),('Docker Compose ps','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/compose/ps/'),('Docker Compose logs','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/compose/logs/')],[
 ('docker context show','Confirm the active Docker context','Compare the context with the deployment host. Environment or command-line endpoint overrides may change the actual daemon, so confirm the approved operating context before drawing conclusions.'),
 ('docker compose config --services','List services in the effective model','Run from the intended project and compare the resulting service names with deployment documentation. File selection, profiles and environment can change the model.'),
 ('docker compose config --quiet','Validate the effective Compose model','This checks whether Compose can parse and resolve the model without printing it. A valid model does not prove that images, networks, credentials or application dependencies work.'),
 ('docker compose ps --all','Inspect project container states','Compare the service name, state and exit information with the expected project. This view depends on the selected project; an empty result does not establish that no containers exist on the daemon.'),
 ('docker compose logs --since 30m --tail 100 --timestamps web','Inspect a bounded service log window','Replace web with the affected service and correlate the first relevant error with the failure time. Logging driver and retention settings affect availability; redact secrets before sharing.')],
 ('A Compose service exits after a configuration change','A web service exits immediately after a deployment configuration update.',[(0,'Confirm the active daemon and intended Compose project before investigation.'),(1,'List effective services using the same files and project context as the deployment.'),(3,'Capture the affected container state and exit information.'),(4,'Review the bounded service logs around the first failed start.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the intended project contains web, its container exits, and its logs report a missing required setting.',
 'A missing application setting is the leading hypothesis in this example. Compose model validation alone cannot establish that application-level settings are sufficient.',
 'After the owner corrects the deployment through the approved process, verify stable container state and a normal service request.',
 'Provide context, project, service name, exit time and a sanitized error excerpt to the application owner.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-10'

guide('Linux memory pressure investigation','Linux','Infrastructure',
 'Compare available memory, swap, process usage and kernel messages when a Linux host slows down.',
 'Linux with procps utilities and systemd journal access. Container limits can differ from host totals.',
 'Use authorized read access on the affected host. Commands provide point-in-time evidence and do not clear caches, kill processes or change memory limits.',
 [('procps free manual','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html'),('procps ps manual','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html'),('systemd journalctl manual','https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/journalctl.html')],[
 ('free -h','Inspect memory and swap totals','Compare the available memory estimate and swap use with the host baseline. Low free memory by itself is not proof of pressure because the kernel also uses memory for cache.'),
 ('cat /proc/meminfo','Read detailed kernel memory accounting','Inspect MemAvailable, SwapTotal and SwapFree alongside the host’s context. Values are point-in-time counters; compare repeated samples and monitoring rather than inferring a trend from one reading.'),
 ('ps -eo pid,comm,rss --sort=-rss','List processes by resident memory','Compare resident set sizes and identify process owners. RSS values are not additive accounting of unique memory because shared pages can be counted in multiple processes.'),
 ('journalctl -k --since "1 hour ago" -n 100 --no-pager','Inspect recent kernel messages','Look for memory-related events within the incident window. An absent message does not exclude prior pressure if journal permissions, retention or the selected time window hide it.'),
 ('systemctl --failed --no-pager','List systemd units in failed state','Check for failed services around the incident. A failed unit does not by itself establish an out-of-memory cause; correlate service and kernel logs with timestamps.')],
 ('A server slows down and a service exits','An application stops responding during a period of unusually high host memory consumption.',[(0,'Capture available memory and swap usage at the time of the incident.'),(2,'Identify the highest resident-memory processes and their owners.'),(3,'Check the kernel journal for memory-related events during the same window.'),(4,'Inspect failed unit status and correlate it with the application timeline.')],
 'Illustrative findings: available memory drops sharply and the kernel journal records an out-of-memory kill affecting the application.',
 'The kernel event supports a memory-kill diagnosis for that process. Determine whether workload growth, limits or another cause led to the pressure before changing sizing.',
 'After the owner applies an approved correction, monitor available memory and application stability over a comparable workload period.',
 'Send timed memory samples, affected process identity and relevant redacted kernel messages to the system and application owners.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-10'

guide('OpenSSH client connection investigation','OpenSSH','Cybersecurity',
 'Inspect OpenSSH client configuration, host keys and verbose connection stages without weakening host verification.',
 'OpenSSH client on a workstation with authorized access to the target host.',
 'Replace admin and server.example.com with your approved account and host. Connection attempts contact the server. Verify host-key fingerprints through a trusted independent channel before accepting any change.',
 [('OpenSSH client manual','https://man.openbsd.org/ssh.1'),('OpenSSH client configuration manual','https://man.openbsd.org/ssh_config.5'),('OpenSSH ssh-keygen manual','https://man.openbsd.org/ssh-keygen.1')],[
 ('ssh -V','Identify the client version','Record the OpenSSH client version when investigating configuration or algorithm compatibility. A version alone does not establish which algorithms the server offers.'),
 ('ssh -G server.example.com','Inspect effective client configuration','Read the resolved host name, port, user and identity settings after client configuration is applied. This is a local configuration inspection; it does not connect or prove the destination is reachable.'),
 ('ssh-keygen -F server.example.com','Find known-host entries','Inspect existing known_hosts matches for the target. An entry or fingerprint mismatch requires verification through an independent trusted channel; do not automatically remove the existing key.'),
 ('ssh -vvv admin@server.example.com','Inspect a connection attempt','Review verbose connection, host-key and authentication stages to locate the failure. Logs can reveal usernames, network topology and key paths, so sanitize them before sharing.'),
 ('ssh -o ConnectTimeout=10 admin@server.example.com','Attempt a bounded connection','Use an authorized account and expected host name. ConnectTimeout limits connection establishment and the initial handshake, not necessarily every later interactive authentication step.')],
 ('SSH fails after a server migration','An administrator sees a host-key warning when connecting to a recently migrated server.',[(0,'Record the client version and operating environment before comparing behavior.'),(1,'Inspect the effective destination, port and user to ensure the intended host is selected.'),(2,'Find the existing known-host entry and compare its fingerprint with trusted inventory.'),(3,'If appropriate, capture the connection stage without accepting an unverified replacement key.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the resolved destination is correct, but its presented host key differs from the previously recorded key.',
 'A mismatch may be legitimate after migration or indicate an unexpected endpoint. The warning alone cannot distinguish those cases; confirm the new fingerprint out of band.',
 'After the administrator verifies and updates host-key trust using the approved process, reconnect with verification enabled and confirm the intended server identity.',
 'Provide the hostname, resolved address and independently verified fingerprints to the server owner; do not share private keys.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-10'

guide('Windows firewall policy inspection','Windows','Cybersecurity',
 'Inspect active Windows firewall profiles, rules and port filters when investigating a blocked connection.',
 'Windows with the NetSecurity PowerShell module; policy visibility depends on permissions.',
 'Run on the affected endpoint with authorized read access. Replace the example rule display name. These commands inspect policy and do not change rules.',
 [('Microsoft firewall profiles','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/netsecurity/get-netfirewallprofile'),('Microsoft firewall rules','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/netsecurity/get-netfirewallrule'),('Microsoft port filters','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/netsecurity/get-netfirewallportfilter')],[
 ('Get-NetConnectionProfile','Identify network connection profiles','Record the interface and network category for the affected path. An endpoint can have multiple interfaces with different categories, so identify which one carries the incident traffic.'),
 ('Get-NetFirewallProfile -PolicyStore ActiveStore','Inspect effective profile settings','Compare enabled state and default inbound and outbound actions for the active profile. Profile settings alone do not evaluate every rule, application filter or IPsec condition.'),
 ('Get-NetFirewallRule -PolicyStore ActiveStore -Enabled True -Direction Inbound','List enabled inbound rules','Review relevant rule names, actions and profiles in the active policy store. A matching display name does not establish that every address, port or program filter matches the connection.'),
 ("Get-NetFirewallRule -PolicyStore ActiveStore -DisplayName 'Example service' | Get-NetFirewallPortFilter",'Inspect ports for a selected rule','Replace the display name with the confirmed rule. Check protocol and local and remote port filters, then separately inspect address, program, service and profile conditions.'),
 ("Get-NetFirewallRule -PolicyStore ActiveStore -DisplayName 'Example service' | Get-NetFirewallAddressFilter",'Inspect address restrictions for a selected rule','Compare allowed local and remote address ranges with the actual flow. Effective policy can include multiple rules, block rules and other conditions; this is not a complete effective-access calculation.')],
 ('A service is reachable from one subnet only','A Windows service accepts connections from one approved subnet but not another.',[(0,'Identify the network profile on the interface carrying the affected connection.'),(1,'Review effective defaults for that profile in the active store.'),(3,'Check the relevant service rule protocol and port filter.'),(4,'Compare the rule remote address filter with both client subnets.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the rule covers the correct TCP port but its remote address filter includes only the working subnet.',
 'The address restriction is a supported explanation in this example. Other matching block rules or network controls still need consideration before changing policy.',
 'After the policy owner approves a scoped correction, test from both authorized subnets and inspect the active rule again.',
 'Provide the endpoint, interface profile, connection tuple and relevant sanitized rule filters to the security owner.'))
NEW_GUIDES[-1]['updated']='2026-11-11'

guide('Kubernetes persistent volume claim investigation','Kubernetes','Cloud',
 'Investigate a pending persistent volume claim using claim events, storage classes and bound volumes.',
 'Kubernetes cluster with kubectl read access to PVCs, pods and relevant storage resources.',
 'Replace production and data-web with the actual namespace and claim. Confirm cluster context and use authorized read access; these commands do not delete claims, volumes or workloads.',
 [('Kubernetes persistent volumes','https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/'),('Kubernetes storage classes','https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/'),('kubectl describe','https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_describe/')],[
 ('kubectl config current-context','Confirm the cluster context','Compare the locally named context with the authorized cluster inventory. Similar local names can refer to different environments, so establish the target before interpreting resources.'),
 ('kubectl get pvc data-web -n production -o wide','Inspect the claim status','Record status, storage class, requested size and any bound volume. A Pending claim is a symptom; its events and binding mode provide the next clues.'),
 ('kubectl describe pvc data-web -n production','Inspect claim details and events','Read the requested access modes, storage class and recent provisioning events. Some classes wait for a consumer to be scheduled before binding; Pending can therefore be an intentional intermediate state.'),
 ('kubectl get storageclass','List storage classes and binding modes','Identify the claim’s named storage class and its volume binding mode. A missing default class or a different class name can lead to different provisioning behavior.'),
 ('kubectl get pods -n production -o wide','Inspect workload scheduling','Identify the pod using the claim and inspect its scheduling state. With WaitForFirstConsumer, placement and storage topology influence provisioning; do not treat an unbound claim in isolation as a failure.'),
 ('kubectl get pv','Inspect persistent volumes','If the claim is bound, match its volume name to the PV and compare capacity, access mode and reclaim policy. A PV object does not prove that a filesystem is mounted or the backing storage is healthy.')],
 ('A workload is pending with an unbound claim','A new application pod waits while its data claim remains Pending.',[(0,'Confirm cluster and namespace before inspecting the affected workload.'),(1,'Capture the exact claim status and storage class name.'),(2,'Read recent claim events and requested access modes.'),(3,'Compare the storage class binding mode with the pod scheduling state.')],
 'Illustrative findings: the claim uses WaitForFirstConsumer and the consumer pod cannot be scheduled because no node satisfies its placement constraints.',
 'The claim waiting for scheduling is consistent with the configured binding mode in this example. Resolve the scheduling evidence before assuming the storage provisioner is broken.',
 'After the workload owner resolves the approved scheduling issue, verify the claim binds, the pod starts and the application can access its expected data.',
 'Provide namespace, claim name, storage class, binding mode and sanitized events to the platform and storage owners.'))
